what is SDK?



SDK stands for Software Development Kit.
In everyday terms, it’s a bundle of tools, code libraries, documentation, and examples that developers use to add a service into an app or website without building everything from scratch.
In your screenshot, Maps SDK usually means Google gives developers ready-made tools to put maps into an app, especially:
Android apps
iPhone/iPad apps
sometimes web-based development kits depending on the product
So instead of manually building map rendering, zoom, markers, and interactions, the SDK handles that for you.
For what you’re talking about — grabbing a front photo of a property and charging for it later — you’re probably not looking for the SDK. You’re more likely looking at:
Street View Static API = returns a still image
Street View Metadata API = tells you whether imagery exists before you request it
possibly Maps Embed if you only want to display an interactive map on a page
So the short version is:
SDK = developer toolkit for building map features into apps
Static API = best for generating a single property image
Embed = best for showing a map on a page
Metadata = best for checking if a photo is available before charging
For your use case, the likely combo is:
check availability with Street View Metadata
after payment, fetch the image with Street View Static API
save it to your storage
show it in the listing/report
