Good morning. Short answer: yes, there are already court cases involving AI, but the law is still developing. Right now, courts generally do not treat AI as its own legal person. In most situations, AI is treated more like a tool used by a human or company. That means if an AI makes a mistake, the legal focus usually lands on the person, business, developer, deployer, or operator behind it, depending on the facts.
So no, AI is usually not considered the same legal entity as the person directing it. But the person directing it can still be responsible for what it does, especially if they relied on it, published its output, acted on it, or failed to review it. A court will usually ask questions like: Who built it? Who deployed it? Who controlled it? Who benefited from it? Who had the duty to check the result? In that sense, AI is not “the same as” the user, but the user may still carry liability because the AI acted as their instrument.
A practical way to think about it is this: if AI helps draft a contract, medical note, listing description, legal filing, or recommendation, the human professional or company using it is still usually expected to review and stand behind it. There can also be shared liability in some cases — for example, between the software company and the business using the software — but courts are not generally saying, “the AI itself is the responsible party.” If you want, I can give you a plain-English breakdown by scenario next: business use, real estate use, legal filings, medical use, and autonomous systems.





