A static “verified” graphic alone won’t be enough. If it’s just an image, people can screenshot it, reuse it, or fake it. The safer model is: graphic + live verification data + server-side status check. The badge should look polished visually, but the trust comes from the fact that it is backed by your database and can be validated in real time.
What I’d recommend is a dynamic verification system:
each verified agent/company gets a unique verification record ID
the badge displays a live status from your system
clicking the badge opens a verification details page
the page shows things like:
verified name
verified entity type: agent or company
issue date
current status
optional expiration/review date
unique verification ID
if status changes, the badge and page update automatically everywhere
That’s what makes it harder to duplicate. Someone can copy the look, but they can’t fake the live record if your reports and share pages always pull the badge state from your backend. To make it stronger, use:
a database-backed boolean/status
a verification token or public ID
a click-through verification URL
optional timestamp / last reviewed date
optional QR code pointing to the live verification page
For a “constant verified” setup, don’t hardcode the badge into images. Instead, render it conditionally anywhere the user appears:
report pages
share pages
profile cards
agent vault
company profiles
Then your logic becomes simple:
if user status = approved → show GRTP Verified badge
if company status = approved → show Company Verified badge
if pending/denied → show the appropriate pending/denied state or nothing
